WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:10.420
Of all the dark songs by the band Nirvana and, there are a lot to choose from, the most disturbing to me is

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.280
Polly, the sixth track of their album,

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:19.590
Nevermind, an acoustic song, a quiet song, about the rape and torture of a teenage girl

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.249
Told from the perspective of the rapist

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.560
It's a hard song to talk about, harder because it's based on a real incident in 1987,

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.460
Gerald Friend, after being paroled early from a similar conviction in 1960,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.599
abducted a 14 year old girl after leaving a concert, brought her back to his mobile home,

00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:44.969
and repeatedly raped her and tortured her with a blowtorch, while she was suspended from a pole.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.040
Yeah, it's horrific.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.980
It's also right in the center of one of the best selling albums of the 1990s.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.720
An album that enjoyed

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.489
mainstream success

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.119
Cruelties like those detailed in Polly are so

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.269
heart-rending that the instinct is to turn away.

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:12.489
The truth of violence like that is easily

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.320
repressed, forgotten, avoided,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.820
but Kurt Cobain couldn't bear that.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.500
In addition to exploring his own fears and anxieties in Nirvana's music,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:26.980
he was also a vocal feminist who repeatedly spoke out against sexism and violence against women,

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.620
especially sexual violence.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.420
It doesn't seem like much but you know, we raised like

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.529
$50,000 from the Bosnian rape victims, you know, and that's a lot more than we could have done

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:40.929
griping about it and talking about it in interviews and like maybe putting out a fanzine and you know

00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:47.440
There's nothing wrong with doing stuff like that. But you know, we're using our, we're using the tools that we've, we have, you know.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.600
Cobain did more than speak.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.140
Nirvana played that benefit concert for Bosnian rape victims and in the lighter notes to incest aside,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:59.644
Cobain instructed sexists not to buy Nirvana albums or come to the shows,

00:01:59.644 --> 00:02:01.680
putting morals before money.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.280
But though Cobain's views were inherently political,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.460
His genius was artistic,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.500
and what an artist can do is different than what an activist or a politician can do.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.850
Art, especially music, impacts us emotionally.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:21.860
It strikes at the core, and as a result, has a unique power to expand our empathy,

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.880
which is why the perspectives of Polly is so shocking.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.463
But before I get to that, let me talk a little bit about the structure of the song.

00:02:29.463 --> 00:02:30.960
I came to Nirvana pretty late.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.209
I had just turned 3 when Nevermind was released

00:02:34.209 --> 00:02:38.190
But I didn't really start listening to their music until a couple years ago.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.599
And the two things that surprised me most were

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:46.920
One, the poetic brilliance of Cobain and, two, the catchiness and simplicity of the songs

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.520
and you don't get much simpler than Polly.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:55.660
Composed in E minor, the chord progression is just the same: E minor, G, D, C for the verses.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.960
Over and over again then

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:01.859
Then G5, B flat 5, D5, E5 for the choruses.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:07.829
In the chorus, an unobtrusive bass enters on the low end and Cobain adds a vocal harmony on a high end.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.890
Don't cut yourself.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.010
I want some help.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:12.890
To please myself.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:17.610
There are no drums in the song besides one cymbal crash at the start of each chorus,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:24.899
Oh and there's a bass break after the second chorus that lasts all of 9 seconds before the repetitive sequence starts up again.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:28.529
So, Polly is basically Cobain and his guitar.

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:31.000
A gentle, catchy  melody and vocal.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.360
and the effect of this restraint is a feeling of closeness, of intimacy, authenticity.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.055
The feeling is heightened even further by the equipment.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.720
On Polly, Cobain played Estela guitar bought for

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.000
$30 at a pawn shop.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.820
"It barely stays in tune", he told Guitar World in 92.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:48.330
"In fact,

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:52.052
I have to use duct tape to hold the tuning keys in place."

00:03:52.052 --> 00:03:53.399
The flat plucky sound

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.960
removes any polish that could mediate between, and ultimately, divide

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.980
musician and listener.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.240
Polly is a song designed, in other words, to give Cobain access to your head.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.680
The question, is once he's in there,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:08.500
what does he do?

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.380
The surprising and profound choice Cobain makes in Polly, is to make the

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:17.420
protagonist of the song, the intimate singer: the rapist.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.280
This becomes very clear after just the first two lines.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.620
Polly wants a cracker

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.869
I think I should get off her first

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.830
We are inside the mind of this vile man,

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.340
Carried along in the stream of his consciousness.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.300
In each verse he reports what's happening with an amused detachment,

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.900
that is somehow more scary for its lack of emotion.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:45.640
Cobain's vocal is aloof yet mesmerized.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.940
Cobain routinely downplayed the importance of his lyrics.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.180
But, don't buy it.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:09.040
While it may be wrong to look for hidden meanings and things like that, his journals reveal a fascination

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.340
with language and word play.

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:16.100
The chorus of Polly is made up of fragments of images and sentiments

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:21.500
that simulate perfectly the chaotic flux of thinking.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.739
You can see desires, regrets, cries for help, cruelty

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.240
self-deception, righteousness,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.660
All momentarily bobbing to the surface, and then,

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.500
the animalistic final thought dictating this man's actions:

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.180
Please myself.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.840
If you're being carried along by the rapist's stream-of-consciousness, in the verses, in the chorus,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.460
you're drowning in it.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.520
Haven't you?

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.140
Let me take a ride, cut yourself

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:47.140
I want some help,

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:48.800
Please myself

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:55.620
In a way that only music can, Cobain forces an emotional identification with evil,

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:57.040
to stop us from

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:58.620
suppressing this brutality.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:05:59.580
to stop us from

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.020
evading it.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.180
He makes it our own. It's a deeply distressing experience,

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.220
but it's also a motivating one.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:10.100
"The problem with groups who deal with rape is that they try to educate women

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:14.340
about how to defend themselves", Cobain told NME in 91.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.060
What really needs to be done is teaching men

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:18.700
not to rape.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.080
Go to the source and start there.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.040
With a catchy tune and a $30 guitar,

00:06:23.559 --> 00:06:25.559
That's just what he does.

